WEB5-012-001,CDC Biosafety Quick Learn,培训,安全规范,生物,3,生物;综合安全,How to explain biosafety levels and their practical differences,How to explain biosafety levels and their practical differences有哪些要点？,"Title: Recognize the four Biosafety Levels URL Source: https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/ Markdown Content: # CDC LC Quick Learn: Recognize the four Biosafety Levels [Skip to Content](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#tab-nav) [![Image 4: Home](http://m.cdc.gov/Content/images/newLargeHeader_noBarTrans.png)](http://m.cdc.gov/en/) [CDC Home](http://www.cdc.gov/ ""Return to the CDC Home Page"") ![Image 5: CDC 24/7: Saving Lives. Protecting People.](https://www.cdc.gov/TemplatePackage/images/cdcHeaderLogo.gif)[CDC 24/7: Saving Lives. Protecting People.](http://www.cdc.gov/ ""CDC 24/7: Saving Lives. Protecting People."") # Quick Learn Lesson # Recognizing the Biosafety Levels * [1](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#tabs-1) * [2](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#tabs-2) * [3](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#tabs-3) * [4](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#tabs-4) * [5](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#tabs-5) * [6](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#tabs-6) * [7](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#tabs-7) * [8](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#tabs-8) * [9](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#tabs-9) * [10](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#tabs-10) * [11](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#tabs-11) * [12](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#tabs-12) * [13](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#tabs-13) * [14](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#tabs-14) * [15](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#tabs-15) * [16](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#tabs-16) * [17](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#tabs-17) * [18](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#tabs-18) ### Recognizing the Biosafety Levels This Quick Learn Lesson will take approximately 15 minutes to complete. When you have completed this lesson, you will be able to recognize characteristics of the four biological safety levels. ![Image 6: Biohazard sign for a biosafety level 2 laboratory. It states biosafety level 2, authorized personnel only.](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/images/biohazard.png) [Next Page »](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) ### Introduction Suppose you are a laboratory microbiologist working with a potentially harmful [microbe](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#). Precautions must be taken in the laboratory to make sure you and others are not infected. * Where in the lab would you complete your work? * What protective equipment and practices would you use? * How would you contain the microbe to limit contamination or accidental infection? These are just a few of the questions that can be answered through an understanding of biosafety and the four biosafety levels (BSLs). [Next Page »](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) [« Prev Page](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) ### Biosafety and Biosafety Levels **What is Biosafety?** Biosafety is the application of safety precautions that reduce a laboratorian’s risk of exposure to a potentially infectious microbe and limit contamination of the work environment and, ultimately, the community. **What are Biosafety Levels (BSLs)?** There are four biosafety levels. Each level has specific controls for containment of microbes and biological agents. The primary risks that determine levels of containment are infectivity, severity of disease, transmissibility, and the nature of the work conducted. Origin of the microbe, or the agent in question, and [the route of exposure](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) are also important. Each biosafety level has its own specific containment controls that are required for the following: * Laboratory practices * Safety equipment * Facility construction [Next Page »](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) [« Prev Page](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) ### BSLs, continued The biosafety levels range from BSL-1 to BSL-4. Each biosafety level builds on the controls of the level before it. Every microbiology laboratory, regardless of biosafety level, follows [standard microbiological practices](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#). ![Image 7: Pyramid showing the four BSLs with the lowest risk microbes at the bottom, representing BSL-1, and the highest risk microbes at the top, representing BSL-4.](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/images/standard-micro-practices.png) You will learn about each level on the following screens. [Next Page »](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) [« Prev Page](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) ### BSL-1 ![Image 8: Pyramid showing the four BSLs with the lowest risk microbes highlighted at the bottom, representing BSL-1. Levels 2-4 are grey.](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/images/bsl-1.png) If you work in a lab that is designated a BSL-1, the microbes there are not known to consistently cause disease in healthy adults and present minimal potential hazard to laboratorians and the environment. An example of a microbe that is typically worked with at a BSL-1 is a [nonpathogenic](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) strain of _E. coli_. Specific considerations for a BSL-1 laboratory include the following: Laboratory practices * Standard microbiological practices are followed. * Work can be performed on an open lab bench or table![Image 9: A](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/images/a.png). Safety equipment * [Personal protective equipment](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#)![Image 10: B](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/images/b.png), (lab coats, gloves, eye protection) are worn as needed. Facility construction * A sink must be available for hand washing. * The lab should have doors to separate the working space with the rest of the facility. ![Image 11: Man working in a BSL-1 lab wearing a lab coat, safety glasses, and gloves while pouring liquid into a beaker on an open lab bench.](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/images/ppe.jpg) [Next Page »](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) [« Prev Page](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) ### BSL-2 ![Image 12: Pyramid showing the four BSLs with BSL-2 highlighted. Levels 1, 3, and 4 are grey.](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/images/bsl-2.png) BSL-2 builds upon BSL-1. If you work in a lab that is designated a BSL-2, the microbes there pose moderate hazards to laboratorians and the environment. The microbes are typically [indigenous](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) and associated with diseases of varying severity. An example of a microbe that is typically worked with at a BSL-2 laboratory is _Staphylococcus aureus_. [Next Page »](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) [« Prev Page](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) ### BSL-2, continued In addition to BSL-1 considerations, BSL-2 laboratories have the following containment requirements: Laboratory practices * Access to the laboratory is restricted when work is being conducted. Safety equipment * Appropriate personal protective equipment (PPE) ![Image 13: A](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/images/a.png) is worn, including lab coats and gloves. Eye protection and [face shields](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) can also be worn, as needed. * All procedures that can cause infection from aerosols or splashes are performed within a [biological safety cabinet (BSC)](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#)![Image 14: B](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/images/b.png). * An [autoclave](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) or an alternative method of decontamination is available for proper disposals. Facility construction * The laboratory has self-closing doors. * A sink and [eyewash](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) are readily available. ![Image 15: Woman working in a BSL-2 laboratory. She is wearing gloves, a face shield, a lab coat and is conducting work within a biological safety cabinet.](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/images/bsl-2-lab.jpg) [Next Page »](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) [« Prev Page](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) ### BSL-3 ![Image 16: Pyramid showing the four BSLs with BSL-3 highlighted. Levels 1, 2, and 4 are grey.](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/images/bsl-3.png) BSL-3 builds upon the containment requirements of BSL-2. If you work in a lab that is designated BSL-3, the microbes there can be either indigenous or exotic, and they can cause serious or potentially lethal disease through respiratory transmission. Respiratory transmission is the inhalation route of exposure. One example of a microbe that is typically worked with in a BSL-3 laboratory is _Mycobacterium tuberculosis_, the bacteria that causes tuberculosis. [Next Page »](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) [« Prev Page](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) ### BSL-3, continued In addition to BSL-2 considerations, BSL-3 laboratories have the following containment requirements: Laboratory practices * Laboratorians are under medical surveillance and might receive immunizations for microbes they work with. * Access to the laboratory is restricted and controlled at all times. Safety equipment * Appropriate PPE must be worn, and [respirators](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) might be required![Image 17: A](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/images/a.png). * All work with microbes must be performed within an appropriate BSC![Image 18: B](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/images/b.png). Facility construction * A hands-free sink and eyewash are available near the exit. * Exhaust air cannot be recirculated, and the laboratory must have sustained directional airflow by drawing air into the laboratory from clean areas towards potentially contaminated areas. * Entrance to the lab is through two sets of self-closing and locking doors![Image 19: C](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/images/c.png). ![Image 20: Man working in a BSL-3 lab wearing PPE, including a powered air purifying respirator. He is working within a BSC. Self-closing locked doors are in the background of the work station.](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/images/bsl-3b-lab.jpg) [Next Page »](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) [« Prev Page](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) ### BSL-4 ![Image 21: Pyramid showing the four BSLs with the highest risk level, BSL-4, highlighted at the top. Levels 1-3 are grey.](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/images/bsl-4.png) BSL-4 builds upon the containment requirements of BSL-3 and is the highest level of biological safety. There are a small number of BSL-4 labs in the United States and around the world. The microbes in a BSL-4 lab are dangerous and exotic, posing a high risk of aerosol-transmitted infections. Infections caused by these microbes are frequently fatal and without treatment or vaccines. Two examples of microbes worked with in a BSL-4 laboratory include Ebola and Marburg viruses. [Next Page »](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) [« Prev Page](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) ### BSL-4, Continued In addition to BSL-3 considerations, BSL-4 laboratories have the following containment requirements: Laboratory practices * Change clothing before entering. * Shower upon exiting. * Decontaminate all materials before exiting. Safety equipment * All work with the microbe must be performed within an appropriate [Class III BSC](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) , or by wearing a full body, air-supplied, [positive pressure](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#)![Image 22: A](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/images/a.png) suit. Facility construction * The laboratory is in a separate building or in an isolated and restricted zone of the building. * The laboratory has dedicated supply and exhaust air, as well as vacuum lines and decontamination systems. ![Image 23: Woman working in a BSL-4 laboratory. She is wearing a full positive pressure suit. No skin is exposed; her air supply can be seen on the back of her suit. She working within a BSC.](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/images/bsl-4-lab.jpg) [Next Page »](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) [« Prev Page](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) ### Your Turn: Exercises Now that you have learned about the four biosafety levels, it's your turn to identify the correct BSL in the examples on the following screens. Select the Next icon to begin. [Next Page »](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) [« Prev Page](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) ### Your turn: Exercise 1 Select the biosafety level described by the conditions of the following example. Levels are listed below. A microbiology graduate student is working on a project under the following conditions: * Work is conducted on a standard laboratory table or bench. * A nonpathogenic laboratory strain of _E. coli_ is being used. * Minimal PPE, such as a lab coat, gloves, and eye protection might be worn but are not necessary. BSL-1 BSL-2 BSL-3 BSL-4 [Next Page »](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) [« Prev Page](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) ### Your turn: Exercise 2 Select the biosafety level shown in the photo below. Click on the photo for a larger image. BSL-1 BSL-2 BSL-3 BSL-4 [![Image 24: Man is wearing a powered air purifying respirator, gloves, and solid-front gown. He is working within a BSC, and self-closing, locked doors are in the background.](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/images/ex-2-a.jpg)](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) [Next Page »](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) [« Prev Page](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) ### Your turn: Exercise 3 Select the biosafety level described by the conditions of the following example. Levels are listed below. * Work is conducted on a standard laboratory bench in a contained area. * PPE, including a lab coat, gloves, and eye protection are being used to reduce accidental infection. BSL-1 BSL-2 BSL-3 BSL-4 [Next Page »](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) [« Prev Page](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) ### Your turn: Exercise 4 Select the biosafety level shown in the following photograph below. Click on the photo for a larger image. BSL-1 BSL-2 BSL-3 BSL-4 [![Image 25: Man is wearing a powered air purifying respirator, gloves, and solid-front gown. He is working within a BSC, and self-closing, locked doors are in the background.](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/images/ex-4-a.jpg)](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) [Next Page »](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) [« Prev Page](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) ### Summary **Congratulations**! You should now be able to recognize characteristics of the four biological safety levels. If you would like additional information or resources on the topic of biosafety, select the Next icon. To view other Quick Learn Lessons, visit [CDC Learning Connection](http://www.cdc.gov/learning). [Next Page »](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) [« Prev Page](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) ### Resources For more information on biosafety and laboratory practice, review the following web resources: * [Biosafety in Microbiological and Biomedical Laboratories (5th Edition)](http://www.cdc.gov/biosafety/publications/bmbl5/index.htm) * [Biosafety — Centers for Disease Control and Prevention](http://www.cdc.gov/biosafety/) * [Respirator Fact Sheet](http://www.cdc.gov/niosh/npptl/topics/respirators/factsheets/respfact.html) [« Prev Page](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) ![Image 26: CDC Learning Connection Logo](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/images/lc_ql_logo.jpg)[For more Quick-Learn lessons please visit CDC Learning Connection](https://www.cdc.gov/learning) [![Image 27: USA.gov: The U.S. Government's Official Web Portal](https://www.cdc.gov/TemplatePackage/images/logo_usagov.gif)](http://www.usa.gov/)[![Image 28: Department of Health and Human Services](https://www.cdc.gov/TemplatePackage/images/logo_dhhs.gif)](http://www.hhs.gov/) Centers for Disease Control and Prevention 1600 Clifton Rd. Atlanta, GA 30333, USA 800-CDC-INFO (800-232-4636) TTY: (888) 232-6348 - [Contact CDC–INFO](https://www.cdc.gov/cdc-info/requestform.html) [![Image 29: Facebook](http://m.cdc.gov/Content/images/FacebookButton.png)](http://www.facebook.com/cdc)[![Image 30: Twitter](http://m.cdc.gov/Content/images/TwitterButton.png)](http://twitter.com/cdcgov)[![Image 31: YouTube](http://m.cdc.gov/Content/images/YouTubeButton.png)](http://www.youtube.com/user/CDCStreamingHealth) ![Image 32: CDC 24/7](http://m.cdc.gov/Content/images/24-7_logo.png) [Full Site](http://www.cdc.gov/) | [Versión en Español](https://www.cdc.gov/es/) [Home](http://m.cdc.gov/en/)[About](http://m.cdc.gov/en/about/about)[Contact Us](http://m.cdc.gov/en/about/contact)[Policies](http://m.cdc.gov/en/about/policies) [U.S. Department of Health and Human Services](http://www.hhs.gov/) Microbe [close](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) **Microbe** Microbes are organisms, such as bacteria and viruses that are too small to be seen by the naked eye. OK Route of Exposure [close](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) **Route of Exposure** Route of exposure is the way a microbe gains access to a living organism. There are four main routes of exposure * Percutaneous, though broken or damaged skin * Inhalation * Mucous membranes of the eyes, nose, and mouth * Ingestion OK Standard Microbiological Practices [close](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) **Standard Microbiological Practices** Standard microbiological practices are those practices that are common to all laboratories. These practices may include * Not eating, drinking, or applying cosmetics in the lab * Washing hands after working with infectious materials and before leaving the lab * Routinely decontaminating work surfaces OK Nonpathogenic [close](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) **Nonpathogenic** A nonpathogenic microbe is one that is not capable of causing disease. OK Personal Protective Equipment (PPE) [close](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) **Personal Protective Equipment (PPE)** Equipment worn to minimize exposure to a variety of hazards. Examples of PPE include laboratory coats, gowns, gloves, eye protection, face shields, shoe covers, and respirators. OK Indigenous [close](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) **Indigenous** Indigenous microbes are those that are commonly found in the geographic region. OK Autoclave [close](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) **Autoclave** Equipment used to decontaminate biological hazardous waste or to sterilize equipment and supplies by subjecting them to high pressure and saturated steam OK Biological Safety Cabinet (BSC) [close](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) **Biological Safety Cabinet (BSC)** A ventilated cabinet designed to provide personnel, product, and environmental protection when appropriate practices and procedures are followed OK Eyewash [close](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) **Eyewash** An apparatus used to physically wash the eyes if they are contaminated by infectious microbes, foreign materials, or other substances OK Face Shields [close](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) **Face Shields** A protective device commonly used to shield the wearer's face and eyes from hazards such as the splashing, spraying, or splattering of potentially harmful infectious materials OK Respirator [close](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) **Respirator** A protective device that covers the nose and mouth or the entire face or head. Lab respirators filter out infectious or harmful particles; some supply the wearer with HEPA-filtered air. Appropriate respirators are chosen based on the type of work being performed. OK Class III BSC [close](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) **Class III BSC** A gas-tight sealed container that is designed to allow for the manipulation of objects, hazardous substances, or infectious disease agents. Class III BSCs have a HEPA filtered air supply and double HEPA filtered exhaust. They provide the ultimate protection for personnel, product, and the environment. OK Positive Pressure Protective Suit [close](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) **Positive Pressure Protective Suit** Highly specialized, totally encapsulating industrial protection garments worn only within special biocontainment or maximum containment (BSL-4) laboratory facilities. The positive pressure in the suit will force air to flow out if the suit is damaged. OK Correct [close](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) **Exercise 1: Correct** That is **correct**! This example suggests a BSL-1 laboratory where there is minimal risk of infection. A nonpathogenic strain of _E. coli_ is being used, and work is conducted on a standard laboratory bench. Special PPE and facility construction are not necessary. OK Incorrect [close](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) **Exercise 1: Incorrect** That is **incorrect**! The correct answer is BSL-1. This example suggests a BSL-1 laboratory where there is minimal risk of infection. A nonpathogenic strain of _E. coli_ is being used, and work is conducted on a standard laboratory bench. Special PPE and facility construction are not necessary. OK Incorrect [close](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) **Exercise 1: Incorrect** That is **incorrect**! The correct answer is BSL-1. This example suggests a BSL-1 laboratory where there is minimal risk of infection. A nonpathogenic strain of _E. coli_ is being used, and work is conducted on a standard laboratory bench. Special PPE and facility construction are not necessary. OK Incorrect [close](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) **Exercise 1: Incorrect** That is **incorrect**! The correct answer is BSL-1. This example suggests a BSL-1 laboratory where there is minimal risk of infection. A nonpathogenic strain of _E. coli_ is being used, and work is conducted on a standard laboratory bench. Special PPE and facility construction are not necessary. OK Exercise2 Image [close](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) ![Image 33: Man is wearing a powered air purifying respirator, gloves, and solid-front gown. He is working within a BSC, and self-closing, locked doors are in the background.](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/images/ex-2-b.jpg) OK Incorrect [close](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) **Exercise 2: Incorrect** That is **incorrect**! The correct answer is BSL-3. This photograph suggests a BSL-3 laboratory. The laboratorian is working within a BSC and is wearing a powered air purifying respirator, gloves, and a solid-front gown. ![Image 34: Man is wearing a powered air purifying respirator, gloves, and solid-front gown. He is working within a BSC, and self-closing, locked doors are in the background.](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/images/ex-2-a.jpg) OK Incorrect [close](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) **Exercise 2: Incorrect** That is **incorrect**! The correct answer is BSL-3. This photograph suggests a BSL-3 laboratory. The laboratorian is working within a BSC and is wearing a powered air purifying respirator, gloves, and a solid-front gown. ![Image 35: Man is wearing a powered air purifying respirator, gloves, and solid-front gown. He is working within a BSC, and self-closing, locked doors are in the background.](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/images/ex-2-a.jpg) OK Correct [close](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) **Exercise 2: Correct** That is **correct**! This photograph suggests a BSL-3 laboratory. The laboratorian is working within a BSC and is wearing a powered air purifying respirator, gloves, and a solid-front gown. ![Image 36: Man is wearing a powered air purifying respirator, gloves, and solid-front gown. He is working within a BSC, and self-closing, locked doors are in the background.](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/images/ex-2-a.jpg) OK Incorrect [close](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) **Exercise 2: Incorrect** That is **incorrect**! The correct answer is BSL-3. This photograph suggests a BSL-3 laboratory. The laboratorian is working within a BSC and is wearing a powered air purifying respirator, gloves, and a solid-front gown. ![Image 37: Man is wearing a powered air purifying respirator, gloves, and solid-front gown. He is working within a BSC, and self-closing, locked doors are in the background.](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/images/ex-2-a.jpg) OK Incorrect [close](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) **Exercise 3: Incorrect** That is **incorrect**! The correct answer is BSL-2. This scenario suggests a BSL-2 laboratory. The laboratorian is working with a pathogenic strain of _Staphylococcus_, which poses moderate risk of infection. Work is conducted on a standard laboratory bench within a contained area, and the laboratorian is wearing appropriate PPE to reduce the risk of accidental infection. OK Correct [close](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) **Exercise 3: Correct** That is **correct**! This scenario suggests a BSL-2 laboratory. The laboratorian is working with a pathogenic strain of _Staphylococcus_, which poses moderate risk of infection. Work is conducted on a standard laboratory bench within a contained area, and the laboratorian is wearing appropriate PPE to reduce the risk of accidental infection. OK Incorrect [close](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) **Exercise 3: Incorrect** That is **incorrect**! The correct answer is BSL-2. This scenario suggests a BSL-2 laboratory. The laboratorian is working with a pathogenic strain of _Staphylococcus_, which poses moderate risk of infection. Work is conducted on a standard laboratory bench within a contained area, and the laboratorian is wearing appropriate PPE to reduce the risk of accidental infection. OK Incorrect [close](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) **Exercise 3: Incorrect** That is **incorrect**! The correct answer is BSL-2. This scenario suggests a BSL-2 laboratory. The laboratorian is working with a pathogenic strain of _Staphylococcus_, which poses moderate risk of infection. Work is conducted on a standard laboratory bench within a contained area, and the laboratorian is wearing appropriate PPE to reduce the risk of accidental infection. OK Exercise4 Image [close](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) ![Image 38: Man is wearing a powered air purifying respirator, gloves, and solid-front gown. He is working within a BSC, and self-closing, locked doors are in the background.](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/images/ex-4-b.jpg) OK Incorrect [close](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) **Exercise 4: Incorrect** That is **incorrect**! The correct answer is BSL-4. This image suggests a BSL-4 laboratory. The scientist is working within a BSC. He is wearing a full-body, air-supplied, positive pressure protective suit. ![Image 39: Man is wearing a powered air purifying respirator, gloves, and solid-front gown. He is working within a BSC, and self-closing, locked doors are in the background.](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/images/ex-4-a.jpg) OK incorrect [close](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) **Exercise 4: Incorrect** That is **incorrect**! The correct answer is BSL-4. This image suggests a BSL-4 laboratory. The scientist is working within a BSC. He is wearing a full-body, air-supplied, positive pressure protective suit. ![Image 40: Man is wearing a powered air purifying respirator, gloves, and solid-front gown. He is working within a BSC, and self-closing, locked doors are in the background.](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/images/ex-4-a.jpg) OK Incorrect [close](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) **Exercise 4: Incorrect** That is **incorrect**! The correct answer is BSL-4. This image suggests a BSL-4 laboratory. The scientist is working within a BSC. He is wearing a full-body, air-supplied, positive pressure protective suit. ![Image 41: Man is wearing a powered air purifying respirator, gloves, and solid-front gown. He is working within a BSC, and self-closing, locked doors are in the background.](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/images/ex-4-a.jpg) OK Correct [close](https://www.cdc.gov/training/QuickLearns/biosafety/#) **Exercise 4: Correct** T