WEB5-018-001,Stanford EHS Lab Safety topic,实验室管理,安全管理,通用,3,综合安全;化学,What routine lab safety controls are required before and during experiments,What routine lab safety controls are required before and during experiments有哪些要点？,"Title: Lab Safety – Stanford Environmental Health & Safety URL Source: https://ehs.stanford.edu/topic/lab-safety Markdown Content: Each laboratory environment poses a unique set of hazards, including chemical, biological, physical, and radiological hazards.The following are general safe laboratory practices for personnel working in labs where hazardous chemicals are used and/or stored. See the[Laboratory Chemical Safety Toolkit](https://ehs.stanford.edu/forms-tools/laboratory-chemical-safety-toolkit)for more information on laboratory and chemical safety.If you’re looking for[information on Toxic Gas](https://ehs.stanford.edu/topic/hazardous-materials/toxic-gas),read about it in the Hazardous Materials topic. ## Understanding hazards * Know the hazards of the chemicals you are working with.Prior to using a chemical with which you are unfamiliar, consult the Safety Data Sheet (SDS) or other appropriate references. * Assume that unknown materials are toxic, and that a mixture is more toxic than its most toxic compound. * Minimize exposure to all chemicals, regardless of toxicity or their familiarity. Most laboratory chemicals have not been fully characterized with respect to their toxicity. It is prudent to implement procedures that will minimize the likelihood of exposure. Skin contact should always be avoided. Avoid inhalation of chemicals and never “sniff” to test chemicals. ## [Research Safety Monthly Spotlight Topics](https://ehs.stanford.edu/wp-content/uploads/Research-Safety-Monthly-Spotlight-Topics.pdf) ## Emergencies Know the location and proper use of emergency equipment such as safety showers, fire extinguishers, and fire alarms. ## Engineering controls Minimize chemical exposure through consistent and proper use of[laboratory fume hoods](https://ehs.stanford.edu/wp-content/uploads/Fume-Hood-Use.pdf), glove boxes, or other ventilated enclosures. ## Personal protective equipment (PPE) When working with hazardous materials or physical hazards, wear: * Personal protective equipment (PPE), including a lab coat, safety glasses, and disposable nitrile gloves (at minimum). Some hazards require additional PPE.See[Personal Protective Equipment](https://ehs.stanford.edu/topic/lab-safety/personal-protective-equipment)for guidance. * Appropriate street clothing, which includes long pants (or equivalent) that cover legs and ankles and non-perforated, closed-toe shoes that completely cover the feet. ## Working alone and unattended operations Working alone should be avoided. Working with hazardous materials and equipment always poses risks to researcher health and safety. However, these risks are heightened when working alone because help is not readily available in case of a mishap. To reduce the risks of working alone: * Do not perform tasks that are not appropriate for working alone. * Reduce the amount(s) of hazardous materials used. * Know the location of, and maintain clear access to, emergency equipment (e.g., safety shower, eye wash, fire extinguisher). * Check alarm systems (e.g., oxygen sensors) frequently and immediately take action upon alarm activation. * Implement a buddy system. For a list of tasks that require PI approval for working alone, tips on how to implement a buddy system and additional information on how to work safely, please see the full [Working Alone Guidance](https://ehs.stanford.edu/topic/lab-safety/working-alone-guidance). ## Avoid ingesting chemicals Do: * Wash your hands frequently to minimize chemical exposure through ingestion and direct contact with the skin. * Always wash hands before eating, drinking, smoking, or applying cosmetics. Don’t: * Use mouth suction for pipetting or siphoning. * Consume or store food or beverages or apply cosmetics in laboratories (including refrigerators and cold rooms) or in chemical storage areas. ## Labeling Label all chemical containers with the identity of the contents. Do not use abbreviations or acronyms. Include hazard warning and chemical concentration information. ## Transporting Use appropriate safety carriers (secondary containment) when transporting chemicals, either inside or outside of the building. ## Lab cleanliness Keep work areas clean and uncluttered.Clean up work areas on completion of an operation or at the end of the day. ## Cold and warm rooms As most controlled temperature rooms (i.e.cold or warm rooms) lack mechanical exhaust (100% recirculated air), storage and use of toxic substances, flammable solvents, corrosive acids, asphyxiants (such as nitrogen and carbon dioxide), and open flames (e.g. Bunsen burners) is strictly prohibited. ## Open Flame Fact Sheets [Bunsen Burner Alternative Products](https://ehs.stanford.edu/wp-content/uploads/Bunsen-Burner-Alternative-Products.pdf) [Open Flames – Safe Practices](https://ehs.stanford.edu/wp-content/uploads/Open-Flames-Safe-Practices.pdf) [Open Flames in Laboratories](https://ehs.stanford.edu/wp-content/uploads/Open-Flames-in-Laboratories.pdf)",,,,,,,基于公开网页资料自动抽取，导入知识库前请人工复核。提取通道=jina；quality_score=0.9618。,Stanford EHS Lab Safety topic | https://ehs.stanford.edu/topic/lab-safety,网页,Stanford EHS Lab Safety topic,Stanford University,,,https://ehs.stanford.edu/topic/lab-safety,2026-03-29,,draft,Stanford;EHS;lab safety,en-US
WEB5-019-001,Stanford EHS Chemical Safety topic,化学,安全管理,化学,3,化学;危化品,What chemical handling and storage rules are highlighted for academic labs,What chemical handling and storage rules are highlighted for academic labs有哪些要点？,"Title: Chemical Safety – Stanford Environmental Health & Safety URL Source: https://ehs.stanford.edu/topic/chemical-safety Markdown Content: # Chemical Safety – Stanford Environmental Health & Safety [Jump to navigation](https://ehs.stanford.edu/topic/chemical-safety#main-menu) [![Image 1: Stanford University](https://www.stanford.edu/su-identity/images/brandbar-stanford-logo@2x.png)](https://www.stanford.edu/) #### For medical, fire or law enforcement assistance, call 911 For EH&S emergencies, including after hours, call (650) 725-9999 ## [![Image 2: Stanford EH&S](https://ehs.stanford.edu/wp-content/themes/suehs/assets/images/logo--stanford.svg)](https://ehs.stanford.edu/ ""Home"") [Menu](javascript:void(0);) Search * [Log In](https://ehs.stanford.edu/saml_login) * [Contact Us](https://ehs.stanford.edu/contact) * [Report an Incident (SU-17)](https://su17.stanford.edu/) #### For medical, fire or law enforcement assistance, call **911** For EH&S emergencies, including after hours, call **(650) 725-9999** * [Contact Us](https://ehs.stanford.edu/contact) * [Report an Incident (SU-17)](https://su17.stanford.edu/) * [Roles](https://ehs.stanford.edu/role) * [Health & Safety Topics](https://ehs.stanford.edu/topic) * [Services](https://ehs.stanford.edu/services) * [Training](https://ehs.stanford.edu/training) * [Guides & Manuals](https://ehs.stanford.edu/manual) * [Standard Operating Procedures](https://ehs.stanford.edu/standard-operating-procedure) * [Forms & Tools](https://ehs.stanford.edu/forms-tools) * [Learning Library](https://ehs.stanford.edu/learning-library) * [Log In](https://ehs.stanford.edu/saml_login) * [Home](https://ehs.stanford.edu/) * [Health & Safety Topics](https://ehs.stanford.edu/topic) * Chemical Safety [Previous Topic](https://ehs.stanford.edu/topic/campus-safety)[Next Topic](https://ehs.stanford.edu/topic/construction-maintenance) # Chemical Safety [Print](javascript:window.print()) [Email](https://ehs.stanford.edu/#email ""Email"")[Copy Link](https://ehs.stanford.edu/#copy_link ""Copy Link"") Hazardous materials, such as chemicals or compressed gases, pose a wide range of health and physical hazards. Health hazards range from mild irritation to carcinogenicity.Physical hazards, such as flammability and reactivity, can also have serious consequences. This section provides information on best practices for the use and storage of chemicals for laboratories. Cal/OSHA regulations require all laboratories using hazardous chemicals to take specific safety measures to ensure safe lab operations. This topic provides guidance to PIs, laboratory supervisors,and lab personnel on compliance with the institutional[Chemical Hygiene Plan (CHP)](https://ehs.stanford.edu/manual/chemical-hygiene-plan).If you’re looking for[information on Toxic Gas](https://ehs.stanford.edu/topic/hazardous-materials/toxic-gas),read about it in the Hazardous Materials topic. ### Responsibilities It is the joint responsibility of the PI or laboratory supervisor and the laboratory personnel to implement the CHP. (Review laboratory safety responsibilities on the[Principal Investigator](https://ehs.stanford.edu/role/principal-investigators)and[Lab Manager](https://ehs.stanford.edu/role/lab-managers)role pages.) Review this topic, as well as the[Lab Safety](https://ehs.stanford.edu/topic/lab-safety)topic, to establish the following key elements of the institutional CHP: * Safety training * General lab practices * Standard Operating Procedures * Required work approvals * Laboratory inspections * Emergency procedures ### Creating a Laboratory-Specific Chemical Safety Plan: To aid in compiling your lab’s safety information, you have the option to create a Laboratory-Specific Chemical Safety Plan. To do so: 1. Print out a[Laboratory Chemical Safety Plan](https://ehs.stanford.edu/wp-content/uploads/lab-chemical-safety-plan.pdf). (Note that this document is pre-formatted for double-sided printing.) 2. Add on lab-specific safety documents, which can be created using the Toolkit (e.g.standard operating procedures, required work approvals, safety training records, and laboratory inspections records). EH&S is available to assist with preparation and/or review of your Laboratory-Specific Chemical Safety Plan. Please contact the Safety & Compliance Officer for your building. #### [Chemical Safety](https://ehs.stanford.edu/topic/chemical-safety) * [Chemical Inventory Management](https://ehs.stanford.edu/topic/chemical-safety/chemical-inventory-management) * [Chemical Waste Disposal](https://ehs.stanford.edu/topic/chemical-safety/chemical-waste-disposal) * [Restricted Chemicals and High Risk Procedures](https://ehs.stanford.edu/topic/chemical-safety/restricted-chemicals-high-risk-procedures) * [Safety Training](https://ehs.stanford.edu/topic/chemical-safety/safety-training) * [Stanford University Controlled Substances Program](https://ehs.stanford.edu/topic/chemical-safety/controlled-substances-and-precursor-chemicals) * [Storage Groups](https://ehs.stanford.edu/topic/chemical-safety/storage-groups) #### Relevant For * [Lab Managers](https://ehs.stanford.edu/role/lab-managers) * [Lab Researchers](https://ehs.stanford.edu/role/lab-researchers) * [Lab Safety Coordinators](https://ehs.stanford.edu/role/lab-safety-coordinators) * [Principal Investigators](https://ehs.stanford.edu/role/principal-investigators) * [University Safety Partners](https://ehs.stanford.edu/role/university-safety-partners) #### Program Areas * [Biosafety & Biosecurity](https://ehs.stanford.edu/topic/biosafety-biosecurity) * [Emergency Management](https://cardinalready.stanford.edu/) * [Environmental Protection](https://ehs.stanford.edu/about-us/environmental-protection) * [Fire Marshal's Office](https://ehs.stanford.edu/topic/fire-safe